---
import "@fontsource-variable/dm-sans/wght.css";
import TypeSpecimen from "@/components/TypeSpecimen.astro";
import { getPublishedEntries } from "@/lib/content";
import { url } from "@/lib/paths";

const entries = await getPublishedEntries();
const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "attention-is-the-first-material", text: "Attention is the first material" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "the-space-between-actions", text: "The space between actions" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "a-humanist-sans-in-a-technical-system", text: "A humanist sans in a technical system" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "designing-for-the-pause", text: "Designing for the pause" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "a-practical-standard", text: "A practical standard" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "compare-the-typefaces", text: "Compare the typefaces" },
];
---

<TypeSpecimen
  pageTitle="A Quieter Kind of Interface — DM Sans Essay"
  description="An essay on attention, pace, and interface design, set in DM Sans with display and long-form reading samples."
  eyebrow="ESSAY / HUMANIST SANS"
  titleLines={["A Quieter Kind", "of Interface"]}
  lede="A humanist sans can make a technical system feel calmer without removing its precision. This essay uses DM Sans for every heading, paragraph, list, and label so its reading rhythm can be judged at full length."
  glyphLines={[
    "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ",
    "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz",
    "0123456789 & @ # % ? ! / ( ) [ ]",
  ]}
  meta={["DM Sans", "Variable 100-1000", "Latin subset", "Self-hosted"]}
  fontClass="article-prose--dm-sans"
  entries={entries}
  headings={headings}
>
  <section>
    <h2 id="attention-is-the-first-material">Attention is the first material</h2>
    <p>
      Every interface asks for attention before it can offer value. The request may be
      obvious, such as a large button, or subtle, such as a line of text that appears after
      a moment of inactivity. In both cases, the product is spending a resource the user
      cannot recover. Good interface design begins by deciding what deserves that resource
      and what should remain quiet.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is why visual restraint is not the same as emptiness. A calm interface still has
      hierarchy, contrast, and movement. It simply removes competition between elements that
      are trying to speak at the same time. The strongest interface often feels inevitable:
      the next action is visible, the current state is clear, and nothing else demands a
      decision.
    </p>
    <p>
      Typography plays a central role in that balance. A page can contain very little color
      and still feel loud if the type is too heavy, the line length is uncontrolled, or every
      label competes for attention. A typeface with an even rhythm helps the eye understand
      where to begin, how to continue, and when to stop.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-space-between-actions">The space between actions</h2>
    <p>
      Software is often described as a sequence of actions: open a project, create a file,
      run a command, publish a result. The experience also depends on the intervals between
      those actions. Loading states, confirmation messages, transitions, and empty screens
      are not interruptions around the work. They are part of the work.
    </p>
    <p>
      A quiet interface treats those intervals as designed space. It tells the user what is
      happening without adding unnecessary movement. It gives feedback without turning every
      background process into a celebration. It allows a completed action to feel complete,
      then gets out of the way.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        The best interface rhythm is not measured by how many things happen at once. It is
        measured by how clearly each moment leads to the next.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      This rhythm is especially important in tools for creative and technical work. A person
      writing, designing, or debugging needs a stable surface. The interface should provide
      confidence that the work is saved, the data is current, and the next step is available.
      It should not repeatedly ask for attention after those conditions have been met.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="a-humanist-sans-in-a-technical-system">A humanist sans in a technical system</h2>
    <p>
      DM Sans brings warmth to a system without making it informal. Its proportions are
      compact, its counters are open, and its shapes remain clear at small sizes. The family
      moves comfortably between headings, interface labels, and long paragraphs. That range
      lets a product keep one visual voice across documentation and functional screens.
    </p>
    <p>
      A monospaced face creates a strong technical signal because every character occupies
      the same width. DM Sans gives up that fixed grid and restores the natural compression
      of letters such as i, l, and t. The result is a more familiar reading rhythm: words hold
      their shape, lines fit more language, and the eye can move quickly across a paragraph.
    </p>
    <p>
      That familiarity is useful when the interface contains complex information. The type
      does not have to announce the technical nature of the product on every line. The
      structure, labels, values, and interactions can carry that responsibility. Typography
      creates the tone around them.
    </p>
    <h3>Clear at small sizes</h3>
    <p>
      Interface text often lives between twelve and sixteen pixels. At that scale, the most
      important qualities are the height of lowercase letters, the openness of counters, and
      the amount of space inside punctuation. DM Sans keeps those details legible without
      making every word feel large.
    </p>
    <h3>Flexible across weights</h3>
    <p>
      The variable version covers a wide weight range, so hierarchy can change while the
      underlying letterforms stay consistent. A medium heading and a bold label feel related
      because they share the same skeleton. The system can become more assertive without
      changing its identity.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="designing-for-the-pause">Designing for the pause</h2>
    <p>
      There is a practical test for interface calm: remove the labels and imagine the screen
      as a map of emphasis. Which area attracts the eye first, second, and third? If every
      region has similar weight, the user receives no guidance. If one region dominates
      without matching the task, the interface points in the wrong direction.
    </p>
    <p>
      A long article makes this test visible. The heading should be distinct, the first
      paragraph should be easy to enter, and the body should settle into a repeatable line
      length. Lists should interrupt the rhythm only when they need to. A quotation should
      create a pause, not a second competing layout.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same principle applies to product screens. A settings page can be dense, but the
      hierarchy should remain stable. A dashboard can contain many values, but only the
      current decision needs the strongest emphasis. An editor can expose many tools, but
      the writing surface should remain the clearest destination.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Use weight to separate levels of information, not to decorate each level.</li>
      <li>Keep body text at a comfortable measure and reserve wider lines for short labels.</li>
      <li>Let whitespace establish groups before borders and backgrounds do the work.</li>
      <li>Use motion to explain a change in state and then return attention to the content.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="a-practical-standard">A practical standard</h2>
    <p>
      A restrained interface is easier to maintain because it has fewer exceptions. Shared
      type styles, spacing tokens, and interaction states create a system that can grow
      without becoming visually noisy. New components inherit the same pace instead of
      introducing another local language.
    </p>
    <p>
      This standard also improves performance. A small set of self-hosted font files avoids
      requests to third-party services and gives the product control over loading behavior.
      A page can ask the browser for only the weight and character range it needs, while the
      design system keeps a stable typographic contract.
    </p>
    <p>
      Quiet design does not aim to make every product look the same. It aims to make the
      relationship between content and interface easier to understand. A typeface like
      DM Sans supports that goal by giving technical work a human pace without weakening its
      structure.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="compare-the-typefaces">Compare the typefaces</h2>
    <p>
      Read this page against the other specimens at the same browser width. Notice the line
      endings first, then compare the weight of the headings, the spacing between words, and
      the amount of visual noise created by a full paragraph.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><a href={url("/type-specimens/ibm-plex-mono/")}>IBM Plex Mono</a> keeps the fixed grid and adds a technical, editorial tone.</li>
      <li><a href={url("/weekly/2026-w44/")}>Manrope</a> provides a compact geometric rhythm for long-form reading.</li>
      <li><a href={url("/weekly/2026-w43/")}>JetBrains Mono</a> emphasizes code-oriented precision and character separation.</li>
      <li>This page: DM Sans creates a softer humanist rhythm while retaining a clear technical hierarchy.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</TypeSpecimen>
